 @FlowNeffets  Highly recommend visiting Pompeii and Vesuvius vulcano. 
The current Vesuv cone sits inside if the remains of the year 79 eruption. The leftovers of the 79 caldera are astonishing! 
The old Vesuv was much bigger than the current cone, so big that one leftover part is now called Mt. Somma. In the 79 eruption it basically turned itself inside out completely and then crashed in on itself. Crazy to walk and drive trough these remains. Gives you a shivering idea of how powerful such explosive eruptions can be.

